  "notes": "Penn State Erie, The Behrend College. Catalog year 2023 plan extracted via bulletin_scraper.py. QA fix (2026-09-17): builder claimed self_verification_passed=true ('2022-2025 schedule successfully across 24 semesters'), but a fresh re-run of build_full_plan (max_terms=40) showed the plan NEVER schedules CHEM 110 or any course downstream of it (CHEM 111/112/113/210/212/213/227/358/413/431W/440/441/450/451/452/453/472, STAT 401/BMB 402) at all -- it stalls repeatedly re-picking CHEM 494 instead and never terminates within 24 (or even 40) simulated terms. Root cause: CHEM 110's real prereq is MATH 22, which chains through MATH 21/4/3, none of which this year's file included -- the same real prereq gap already documented and fixed in CHEMBH-2026.json's own notes. Added MATH 3/4/21/22 explicitly (Semester 1: MATH 3/4; Semester 2: MATH 21/22), matching CHEMBH-2026.json's fix. After fix: 0 warnings, schedules within 8 terms. QA fix (2026-09-17): builder claimed self_verification_passed=true, but build_full_plan (max_terms=40) never terminates for this plan -- it schedules everything else correctly but then loops forever re-attempting the 'CHEM 494/495' requirement: this year's file has 3 separate 1-credit CHEM-494-family items (one each in Semesters 6/7/8) but only 2 distinct course code(s) to draw from, so once that/those code(s) are marked completed the engine can never fill the remaining identical-option item(s) (it has no concept of a repeatable course). CHEM 496 (Independent Studies, 1cr) is a real, verified course in the same family -- added as a third alternative to all 3 of these items, matching CHEMBH-2026.json's own (working) 3-alternative modeling of the identical degree requirement. After fix: 0 warnings, schedules within 8 terms. QA fix (2026-09-17): 'BMB 402' was modeled as an unconditional hard-required course (options: [BMB 402], credits 3) in Semester 6 -- but a fresh fetch of the actual archived bulletin page's own footnote legend (dl.sc_footnotes) reads verbatim: '3-credits from BMB 402, MATH 250 or STAT 401 are required. Additional credits from this will be used as a supporting course.' This means only ONE 3-credit pick from that group is required at all (already correctly captured by this same plan's Semester 4 'MATH 250 or STAT 401' item) -- the Semester 6 BMB 402 line is the bulletin illustrating that a student who ALSO takes BMB 402 gets it credited as a generic Supporting Course pick, not a second mandatory requirement. Compounding this, BMB 402 itself real-requires BMB 401 (not in this plan's own required courses), so modeling it as unconditional made the whole plan unschedulable. Converted to a generic 'Supporting Course or BMB 402' slot, matching CHEMBH-2022.json's own (correct) modeling of the identical footnoted rule.",
